import "core"


module(System)
  # # Environment
    What a program can read of the environment it was started in. It runs in an [/Catln/IO]
    context because it reaches outside the program: a run started with different variables set
    answers differently, so reading one is an effect, and a program can only do it where it runs in
    an io.
    This is not part of the standard library. A program which reads no variable has no use for it,
    and one that does writes `import "env"`.
  env{io -> IO}(name -> /Catln/CStr) -> /Data/Maybe[$T -> String]{IO}
    # [env] is the value of the environment variable called [name], or [/Data/Maybe/Nothing] where
      no variable of that name is set. A variable set to the empty string is set, and is answered
      with the empty string rather than with nothing.
      What a run sees is decided by whatever started it. A program run by `catln run` sees the
      process's own environment; a test or the webdocs' debugger sees what it was given, which for
      the debugger is nothing at all.
    #runtime("ioEnv")
